{"cid:708857":{"key":"cid:708857","title":"Pyrantel","compound":{"key":"cid:708857","cid":708857,"qid":"Q426897","title":"Pyrantel","iupac":"1-methyl-2-[(E)-2-thiophen-2-ylethenyl]-5,6-dihydro-4H-pyrimidine","wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyrantel","cas":"15686-83-6","iuphar":null,"drugbank":"DB11156","chemspider":"618121","unii":"4QIH0N49E7","kegg":"D08451; C07409","chebi":"8654","chembl":"CHEMBL1626223","pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":"DTXSID5023538","echa":"100.036.143","description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":"P02CC01","mesh":"D011715","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:28+00:00","eml":1,"smiles":"CN1CCCN=C1/C=C/C2=CC=CS2","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":null,"bbw":0,"fda":0,"ema":1,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:42:35+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"For the treatment of enterobiasis including roundworm (ascariasis), pinworm (enterobius) and hookworm (strongyloides) and hookworm (ancylostoma) in the pyrantel pamoate form.  Pyrantel is available in various formulations for humans, dogs, and cats as the pamoate (US Pharmacopeia nomenclature) or embonate (European Pharmacopoeia nomenclature) salt, which contains 34.7% pyrantel base combined with pamoic acid.,.  Pyrantel pamoate (embonate) ingested orally is effective for removal and control of ascarid and hookworm infections in puppies and dogs (adult Toxocara canis, Toxascaris leonina, Ancylostoma tubaeforme, An. braziliense, Uncinaria stenocephala), cats (adult Toxocara cati, Toxa. leonina, An. caninum, An. braziliense, U. stenocephala), horses and ponies (adult and immature Parascaris equorum, adult Strongylus vulgaris, S. edentatus, S. equinus, Cyathostomes (Triodontophorus spp., Cyathostomum spp., Cylicodontophorus spp., Cylicocyclus spp., Cylicostephanus spp., Poteriostomum spp.), Oxyuris equi, Anoplocephala perfoliata), swine (adult Ascaris suum, Oesophagostomum dentatum), and humans (adult A. lumbricoides, Enterobius vermicularis, An. duodenale, Necator americanus).","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB11156","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","items":[{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"Antinematodal Agents; Neuromuscular Depolarizing Agents","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"Pyrantel pamoate is an alternative ... in the treatment of ascariasis and enterobiasis. High cure rates have been achieved after a single oral dose ... Similarly, high cure rates have been obtained against Ancylostoma, N. americanus, and Trichostrongylus.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"IT IS RECOMMENDED...AS AN ALTERNATE DRUG IN TREATMENT OF ROUNDWORM INFECTIONS & IS UNDER INVESTIGATION FOR USE IN TRICHOSTRONGYLUS SPECIES INFECTIONS. /PAMOATE/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"THREE CONSECUTIVE DAILY DOSES ARE GENERALLY MORE EFFECTIVE IN HOOKWORM INFECTIONS. THIS DRUG IS ESSENTIALLY INEFFECIVE AGAINST WHIPWORMS. /PAMOATE/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Substances used in the treatment or control of nematode infestations. They are used also in veterinary practice.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0001481.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Drugs that interrupt transmission at the skeletal neuromuscular junction by causing sustained depolarization of the motor end plate. These agents are primarily used as adjuvants in surgical anesthesia to cause skeletal muscle relaxation.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0014724.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Drug Classes","items":[{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Anthelmintic Agents","source":"LiverTox","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/livertox/Pyrantel/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"It has similar properties to both competitive and depolarizing neuromuscular blocking agents, which leads to the understanding of the paralytic effect of the drug has on parasites, ultimately resulting in the death of the parasite,.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB11156","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"By promoting the release of acetylcholine, inhibiting cholinesterase, and stimulating ganglionic neurons, pyrantel serves as a depolarizing neuromuscular blocking agent in helminths. This causes extensive depolarization of the helminth muscle membrane, resulting in tension to the helminth's muscles, leading to paralysis and release of their attachment to the host organism intestinal walls.  This action is unlike piperazine, which is a hyperpolarizing neuromuscular blocking agent that causes relaxation of the helminth muscles, leading to a subsequent detachment from the intestinal wall. Excretion of the parasites in the feces occurs by normal peristalsis.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB11156","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"PYRANTEL & ITS ANALOGS ARE DEPOLARIZING NEUROMUSCULAR BLOCKING AGENTS. THEY INDUCE MARKED, PERSISTENT ACTIVATION OF NICOTINIC RECEPTORS, WHICH RESULTS IN SPASTIC PARALYSIS OF THE WORM. PYRANTEL ALSO INHIBITS CHOLINESTERASES. /PYRANTEL PAMOATE AND ANALOGS/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"PYRANTEL ... CAUSES A SLOWLY DEVELOPING CONTRACTURE OF PREPARATIONS OF ASCARIS AT 1% OF THE CONCENTRATION OF ACETYLCHOLINE REQUIRED TO PRODUCE THE SAME EFFECT. IN SINGLE MUSCLE CELLS OF THIS HELMINTH, PYRANTEL CAUSES DEPOLARIZATION & INCREASED SPIKE-DISCHARGE FREQUENCY, ACCOMPANIED BY INCREASE IN TENSION.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Pyrantel is poorly absorbed from the GI tract of humans,.  Peak serum concentrations occur 1–3 hours after a single dose.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB11156","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Approximately 50% of an oral dose is excreted unchanged in feces; 7% excreted in urine as unchanged drug and metabolites.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB11156","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"... POORLY ABSORBED FROM GI TRACT ... LESS THAN 15% IS EXCRETED IN THE URINE AS PARENT DRUG & METABOLITES. THE MAJOR PROPORTION OF AN ADMINISTERED DOSE IS RECOVERED IN FECES. /PYRANTEL PAMOATE/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"THOUGH ALMOST COMPLETELY INSOL, THE PAMOATE SALT IS ABSORBED SLIGHTLY; 1% OF UNCHANGED & 3% OF METABOLIZED DRUG APPEAR IN THE URINE. /PAMOATE/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Pyrantel is administered orally. The poor solubility of the pamoate salt offers the advantage of reduced absorption from the gastrointestinal tract and allows the drug to reach and act against parasites in the large intestine. Metabolism of pyrantel is rapid. The absorbed drug is partly metabolized in the liver.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB11156","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"In pigs, following intravenous administration, pyrantel exhibited a half-life of 1.75 +/- 0.19 h.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB11156","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"LiverTox Summary","items":[{"section":"LiverTox Summary","text":"Pyrantel is a nonabsorbed anthelmintic agent with activity against intestinal nematodes such as pinworms and roundworms. Pyrantel therapy has not been reported to cause serum aminotransferase elevations or clinically apparent liver injury.","source":"LiverTox","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/livertox/Pyrantel/"}]},{"section":"Hepatotoxicity","items":[{"section":"Hepatotoxicity","text":"Pyrantel therapy has not been clearly associated with elevations in serum aminotransferase levels nor has its use been linked to cases of clinically apparent liver injury. Likelihood score: E (unlikely cause of clinically apparent liver injury).","source":"LiverTox","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/livertox/Pyrantel/"}]},{"section":"Interactions","items":[{"section":"Interactions","text":"THE EFFECTS OF PYRANTEL ARE BLOCKED BY PREVIOUS EXPOSURE OF ASCARIS MUSCLE TO PIPERAZINE. /PAMOATE/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252"}]}],"manufacturing":[{"section":"Methods of Manufacturing","items":[{"section":"Methods of Manufacturing","text":"Prepn: Belgian patent 658,987 (1965 to Pfizer), CA 64, 8192c (1966); Austin et al, Nature 212: 1273 (1966); Kasubrick, McFarland, South Africa patent 68 00,516 corresp to US patent 3,502,661 (1968, 1970 to Pfizer).","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252"},{"section":"Methods of Manufacturing","text":"THIOPHENE IS CONVERTED TO 2-THIOPHENE-CARBOXALDEHYDE VIA VILSMEIER-HAACK REACTION. N-METHYL-1,3-PROPANEDIAMINE IS CONDENSED WITH ACETONITRILE TO YIELD 1,4,5,6-TETRAHYDRO-1,2-DIMETHYLPYRIMIDINE WHICH IS COUPLED WITH 2-THIOPHENECARBOXALDEHYDE IN PRESENCE OF METHYL FORMATE TO YIELD PYRANTEL (BASE).","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252"},{"section":"Methods of Manufacturing","text":"The compound is synthesized by reacting 2-thiophenealdehyde with 1,2-dimethyltetrahydropyrimidine.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252"}]},{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","items":[{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"CP-10,423-18; Banmith; Strongid /Tartrate/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252"},{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"Pyrantel Embonate; CP-10423-16; Antiminth; Cobantril; Combantrin; Early Bird; Helmex; Helmintox; Piranver /Pamoate/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252"},{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"PYRANTEL PAMOATE, USP ... IS SUPPLIED AS AN OFFICIAL ORAL SUSPENSION: EACH MILLILITER CONTAINS 50 MG OF THE BASE. /PAMOATE/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252"}]},{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","items":[{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","text":"VET: THE US NOW PERMITS ITS USE IN SWINE, BUT LIMITS ITS RESIDUES IN THEIR EDIBLE TISSUES @ 10 PPM FOR LIVER & KIDNEY, & 1 PPM IN MUSCLE. THIS IS ACCOMPLISHED BY WITHHOLDING ANY TREATED FEEDS FOR @ LEAST 24 HR BEFORE SLAUGHTERING. /TARTRATE/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3252"}]}],"structure":true,"populations":{"child":{"label":"Suitable for children","count":5,"forms":[["Chewable Tablets",3],["Unit dose oral suspension",1],["Oral Suspension",1]]},"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":5,"forms":[["Chewable Tablets",3],["Unit dose oral suspension",1],["Oral Suspension",1]]}},"reg":{"fda":0,"ema":1,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":null,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:708857","title":"Pyrantel","products":20,"mw":206.3,"logp":2.5,"tpsa":15.6,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":2,"rings":2,"heavy":14,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C11H14N2S"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:5707","title":"Xylazine","products":5,"mw":220.3,"logp":3.21,"tpsa":24.4,"hbd":1,"hba":3,"rotb":1,"rings":2,"heavy":15,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C12H16N2S","similarity":0.24},{"key":"cid:6726","title":"Cyclizine","products":4,"mw":266.4,"logp":3.02,"tpsa":6.5,"hbd":0,"hba":2,"rotb":3,"rings":3,"heavy":20,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C18H22N2","similarity":0.222},{"key":"cid:16351","title":"Clotiapine","products":1,"mw":343.9,"logp":4.13,"tpsa":18.8,"hbd":0,"hba":4,"rotb":0,"rings":4,"heavy":23,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C18H18ClN3S","similarity":0.217},{"key":"cid:89594","title":"Nicotine","products":8,"mw":162.2,"logp":1.85,"tpsa":16.1,"hbd":0,"hba":2,"rotb":1,"rings":2,"heavy":12,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C10H14N2","similarity":0.216},{"key":"cid:4205","title":"Mirtazapine","products":12,"mw":265.4,"logp":2.48,"tpsa":19.4,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":0,"rings":4,"heavy":20,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C17H19N3","similarity":0.207},{"key":"cid:3827","title":"Ketotifen","products":16,"mw":309.4,"logp":4.01,"tpsa":20.3,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":0,"rings":4,"heavy":22,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C19H19NOS","similarity":0.203},{"key":"cid:135398745","title":"Olanzapine","products":47,"mw":312.4,"logp":3.44,"tpsa":30.9,"hbd":1,"hba":5,"rotb":0,"rings":4,"heavy":22,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C17H20N4S","similarity":0.194},{"key":"cid:3219","title":"Emedastine","products":1,"mw":302.4,"logp":2.21,"tpsa":33.5,"hbd":0,"hba":4,"rotb":5,"rings":3,"heavy":22,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C17H26N4O","similarity":0.19}]},"eml":true,"names":[["PYRANTEL",20]],"atc":[{"code":"P02CC01","path":[{"code":"P","label":"Antiparasitic products, insecticides and repellents"},{"code":"P02","label":"Anthelmintic"},{"code":"P02C","label":"Antinematodal agent"},{"code":"P02CC","label":null},{"code":"P02CC01","label":null}]}],"by_type":{"human":1,"veterinary":19},"co_ingredients":[{"key":"cid:4891","title":"Praziquantel","count":15},{"key":"cid:135449328","title":"Febantel","count":13},{"key":"cid:73169092","title":"Sarolaner","count":3},{"key":"cid:9832912","title":"Moxidectin","count":3}]},"cid:2788":{"key":"cid:2788","title":"Clioquinol","compound":{"key":"cid:2788","cid":2788,"qid":"Q5134338","title":"Clioquinol","iupac":"5-chloro-7-iodoquinolin-8-ol","wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clioquinol","cas":"130-26-7","iuphar":null,"drugbank":"DB04815","chemspider":"2686","unii":"7BHQ856EJ5","kegg":"D03538","chebi":"74460","chembl":"CHEMBL497","pdb_ligand":"CQL","comptox":"DTXSID7022837","echa":"100.004.533","description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":"D08AH30; D09AA10; G01AC02; P01AA02; S02AA05","mesh":"D007464","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:30+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"C1=CC2=C(C(=C(C=C2Cl)I)O)N=C1","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":1934,"bbw":0,"fda":1,"ema":1,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:45:43+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"Used as a topical antifungal treatment.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04815","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","items":[{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"Amebicides; Anti-Infective Agents, Local","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"Clioquinol is used topically in the treatment of tinea pedis, tinea cruris, and other skin infections caused by dermatophytic fungi (ringworm).","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"MEDICATION (VET): has been used as an intestinal anti-infective.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"Topical anti-infective; anti-amebic","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Clioquinol is a broad-spectrum antibacterial with antifungal properties. Application of clioquinol to extensive or eroded areas of the skin may lead to increased protein-bound iodine (PBI) levels within 1 week. In addition, elevated PBI levels may occur when relatively small areas of the skin are treated with clioquinol for more than 1 week.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04815","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Clioquinol is bacteriostatic, however, the precise mechanism of its action is unknown.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04815","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Topical absorption is rapid and extensive, especially when the skin is covered with an occlusive dressing or if the medication is applied to extensive or eroded areas of the skin. Clioquinol is absorbed through the skin in sufficient amounts to affect thyroid function tests.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04815","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Clioquinol is absorbed systemically following topical application to the skin. In 2 studies in which clioquinol combined with a corticosteroid was applied topically to the skin as a cream or ointment, it was estimated that about 2-3% of the dose was absorbed systemically. However, in another study in which the drug was applied alone to the skin as a 3% cream and covered with an occlusive wrap for 12 hr, it was estimated that about 40% of the dose was absorbed percutaneously during this period.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Patients with widespread dermatitis were treated with 15-20 g of 3% clioquinol ointment to 40% of the body area twice daily. The serum concentration increased to 0.8-1.2 ug/ml within 4 hr of application. In one patient, 15-20 mg was excreted in the urine daily mainly in the form of conjugated metabolites. This skin treatment resulted in a urinary excretion somewhat less than is obtained after a daily oral dose of 25 mg (one tablet) of clioquinol. Thus, 3-4% of the applied clioquinol was absorbed. 25% was excreted in the urine.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Clioquinol has known human metabolites that include Clioquinol O-glucuronide.","source":"NORMAN Suspect List Exchange","url":null,"license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"11-14 hr","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"Populations at Special Risk","items":[{"section":"Populations at Special Risk","text":"... Use in those younger than 2 years of age is contraindicated.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843"}]},{"section":"Adverse Effects","items":[{"section":"Adverse Effects","text":"Skin Sensitizer - An agent that can induce an allergic reaction in the skin.","source":"Haz-Map, Information on Hazardous Chemicals and Occupational Diseases","url":null}]},{"section":"Antidote and Emergency Treatment","items":[{"section":"Antidote and Emergency Treatment","text":"Emesis induction is indicated after adult ingestion of 1,500 to 1,800 mg of clioquinol if there is no coma, convulsions, or loss of gag reflex. Otherwise, gastric lavage with tracheal protection, activated charcoal, and cathartics. There are no data on the use of forced diuresis, hemodialysis, peritoneal dialysis, hemoperfusion, or exchange transfusion in the treatment of halogenated 8-hydroxyquinoline poisonings. There are no antidotes.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843"}]}],"manufacturing":[{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","items":[{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"Topical Cream 3%, Iodochlorhydroxyquin Cream, CMC.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843"},{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"Topical Cream, 3% with Hydrocortisone 0.5%, Ala-Quin, Del-Ray; Vioform -Hydrocortisone Mild, Ciba; 3% with Hydrocortisone 1%, Corque, Geneva; Cortin, C & M Pharmacal; Hysone, Mallard; Vioform -Hydrocortisone; Ciba 3% with Hydrocortisone 1% and Pramoxine Hydrochloride 1% 1 + 1-F, Dunhall Lotion, 0.75% with Hydrocortisone 0.25%, UAD Lotion (with parabens and propylene glycol), UAD Laboratories Ointment, 3% with Hydrocortisone 1%, Vioform-Hydrocortisone, Ciba.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843"},{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"Cream and ointment, 3%","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843"},{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"Clipquinol is available in the United States and elsewhere as a 3% ointment or cream and a powder.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/6843"}]},{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","items":[{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","text":"8-Quinolinol, 5-chloro-7-iodo-: ACTIVE","source":"EPA Chemicals under the TSCA","url":"https://www.epa.g